module core.calendar

/// The legal public holidays of 5 U.S.C. § 6103(a), each observed under
/// § 6103(b)(1) and Executive Order 11582 § 3(a) (both set out with the
/// section): a fixed-date holiday on a Saturday is observed the Friday
/// before, on a Sunday the Monday after. FRCP 6(a)(6)(A), FRAP 26(a)(6)(A)
/// and 19 C.F.R. § 201.14(a) all count these days.
///
/// Each holiday is in force from the date the vendored section dates it
/// to. The fixed-date holidays and Labor Day restate former 5 U.S.C. 87
/// (the Act of June 28, 1894), the November 11 holiday former § 87a (Act of
/// May 13, 1938) and Thanksgiving's fourth Thursday former § 87b (Act of
/// December 26, 1941), per the section's Historical and Revision Notes.
/// Earlier history is out of scope: no counting rule here is in force
/// before 1963.
///
/// Observed days. FRCP 6(a)(6)(A) (and before 2009, 6(a)'s "legal
/// holiday") names "the day set aside by statute for observing" each
/// holiday. The Sunday-to-Monday shift is validated by a ruling: July 4,
/// 1993 was a Sunday, and the D.C. Circuit counted "Monday, July 5, 1993"
/// as a legal holiday (Derrington-Bey v. D.C. Dep't of Corrections, 39
/// F.3d 1224, 1225 (D.C. Cir. 1994); sources/change-corpus/rulings, tested
/// in frcp6-history-tests.lit). No ruling in that corpus decides a Friday
/// before a Saturday holiday; it rests on § 6103(b)(1)'s text alone, and
/// its direction of risk is a later due date. The vendored section dates
/// the Saturday rule to former § 87c (Pub. L. 86-362, 1959) and the Sunday
/// rule to Executive Order 11582 § 3(a) (February 11, 1971), which revoked
/// Executive Order 10358 (1952), whose text is not vendored. Before 1971
/// no fixed-date holiday here is observed on another day: a counting rule
/// in force 1963-1971 sees only the holiday itself.
///
/// Not modeled here (FRCP 6(a)(6)(B), (C); FRAP 26(a)(6)(B), (C)): days the
/// President or Congress declares a holiday, which no vendored source
/// lists, and the holidays of the state where the district court sits,
/// which belong to a district's own calendar and, from 2009, extend only
/// forward-counted periods: a state's calendar below (`delaware-holidays`)
/// is read by its own counting rule as `forward_holidays`. Where a last day
/// falls on a day no calendar lists, the date computed here is the earlier
/// one, never a later one.
///
/// The cite is today's § 6103 (2023 ed.), whose credits and amendment
/// notes date each entry's window: a later text, cited for the whole
/// history on purpose.
calendar us-federal-holidays cite "5 U.S.C. § 6103(a)" historical
{
  // Executive Order 11582 § 3(a), February 11, 1971; § 6103(b)(1).
  new-years-day-before-1971: fixed(1, 1) effective [1894-06-28, 1971-02-11)
  new-years-day: fixed(1, 1) effective [1971-02-11, ..) observed nearest_weekday
  // Pub. L. 98-144 § 2: effective "the first January 1 that occurs after the
  // two-year period following" November 2, 1983.
  mlk-day: nth(3, monday, 1) effective [1986-01-01, ..)
  // Pub. L. 90-363 § 2: from January 1, 1971 Washington's Birthday, Memorial
  // Day and Veterans Day move from February 22, May 30 and November 11 to
  // Mondays, and Columbus Day joins the list.
  washingtons-birthday-february-22: fixed(2, 22) effective [1894-06-28, 1971-01-01)
  washingtons-birthday: nth(3, monday, 2) effective [1971-01-01, ..)
  memorial-day-may-30: fixed(5, 30) effective [1894-06-28, 1971-01-01)
  memorial-day: last(monday, 5) effective [1971-01-01, ..)
  // Pub. L. 117-17 § 2, enacted and in force June 17, 2021.
  juneteenth: fixed(6, 19) effective [2021-06-17, ..) observed nearest_weekday
  independence-day-before-1971: fixed(7, 4) effective [1894-06-28, 1971-02-11)
  independence-day: fixed(7, 4) effective [1971-02-11, ..) observed nearest_weekday
  labor-day: nth(1, monday, 9) effective [1894-06-28, ..)
  columbus-day: nth(2, monday, 10) effective [1971-01-01, ..)
  veterans-day-november-11: fixed(11, 11) effective [1938-05-13, 1971-01-01)
  veterans-day-october: nth(4, monday, 10) effective [1971-01-01, 1978-01-01)
  // Pub. L. 94-97: back to November 11, effective January 1, 1978.
  veterans-day: fixed(11, 11) effective [1978-01-01, ..) observed nearest_weekday
  thanksgiving-day: nth(4, thursday, 11) effective [1941-12-26, ..)
  christmas-day-before-1971: fixed(12, 25) effective [1894-06-28, 1971-02-11)
  christmas-day: fixed(12, 25) effective [1971-02-11, ..) observed nearest_weekday
}

/// Before the 2016 time-computation amendments, the federal rules did not
/// include state holidays in the definition of a legal holiday. Keep the
/// shared calendar empty for historical deadline tests; FRCP 6(a)(6)(A)
/// identifies the then-applicable federal holidays.
calendar district-state-holidays effective [1963-07-01, 2016-12-01) cite "FRCP 6(a)(6)(A)" historical
{}

/// Placeholder for the state holidays required by FRCP 6(a)(6)(C),
/// FRCrimP 45(a)(6)(C), and FRBP 9006(a)(6)(C). Each district needs its own
/// list from the applicable state's law or a court rule. No such state-law
/// texts are vendored for
/// those districts, so this calendar deliberately has no dates; until a
/// source is added, a date that depends on a state holiday remains an
/// earlier-date result (the missing source is the district's state-holiday
/// statute/rule). Delaware's are vendored: `delaware-holidays`, read by
/// core's `frcp6-delaware` and `frcrimp45-delaware` (the D. Del. counts).
/// Colorado, Hawaii, Utah and New Mexico districts declare their own sourced
/// lists in their packages (`cod`, `bankr-co`, `bankr-hi`, `utd`, `bankr-ut`,
/// `nmd`, `bankr-nm`). For state statutes not yet vendored, the known holes
/// include California, Arkansas, Georgia, Mississippi, Ohio, Oklahoma and
/// Tennessee (`lg-ovyp`, GitHub #23); the Virgin Islands uses its court rule.
/// The Tenth Circuit BAP (`bap10`) counts Colorado holidays only because a
/// matter does not identify its originating district.
calendar district-state-holidays effective [2016-12-01, ..) cite "FRCP 6(a)(6)(C)" historical
{}

/// RCFC 6(a)(6)(A)'s legal holidays: the § 6103(a) days in force when the
/// RCFC time-computation rules here are (2002 onward), plus Inauguration
/// Day, which FRCP 6(a)(6) does not name. § 6103(c) makes January 20 "of
/// each fourth year after 1965" a holiday, as codified by Pub. L. 89-554
/// (September 6, 1966; the uncodified Pub. L. 85-1 of 1957 preceded it). It
/// states only the Sunday shift; observing a Saturday Inauguration Day on
/// the Friday before, as § 6103(b)(1) does for every other holiday, is the
/// interpretive choice `packs/clock/SOURCES-NOTES.md` records. The cite is
/// today's RCFC 6, whose Rules Committee Notes record its changes since
/// 2002 (Juneteenth added in 2022): a later text, cited for the whole
/// history on purpose.
calendar rcfc-legal-holidays cite "RCFC 6(a)(6)(A)" historical
{
  new-years-day: fixed(1, 1) effective [1894-06-28, ..) observed nearest_weekday
  inauguration-day: every(4, 1965, 1, 20) effective [1966-09-06, ..) observed nearest_weekday
  mlk-day: nth(3, monday, 1) effective [1986-01-01, ..)
  washingtons-birthday: nth(3, monday, 2) effective [1971-01-01, ..)
  memorial-day: last(monday, 5) effective [1971-01-01, ..)
  juneteenth: fixed(6, 19) effective [2021-06-17, ..) observed nearest_weekday
  independence-day: fixed(7, 4) effective [1894-06-28, ..) observed nearest_weekday
  labor-day: nth(1, monday, 9) effective [1894-06-28, ..)
  columbus-day: nth(2, monday, 10) effective [1971-01-01, ..)
  veterans-day: fixed(11, 11) effective [1978-01-01, ..) observed nearest_weekday
  thanksgiving-day: nth(4, thursday, 11) effective [1941-12-26, ..)
  christmas-day: fixed(12, 25) effective [1894-06-28, ..) observed nearest_weekday
}

/// The federal holidays "within the District of Columbia" of 37 C.F.R.
/// §§ 1.7(a) and 2.196: the legal public holidays of 5 U.S.C. § 6103(a), observed under
/// § 6103(b)(1), plus Inauguration Day, which § 6103(c) makes a holiday for
/// federal employees in the District of Columbia area. Unlike the other
/// holidays, § 6103(c) moves only a Sunday Inauguration Day (to Monday); a
/// Saturday one is not observed on Friday (unlike core's
/// `rcfc-legal-holidays`, which observes it then). Effective dates are those
/// of core's `us-federal-holidays`.
calendar dc-federal-holidays effective [2013-12-18, ..) known_at 2026-01-01 cite "37 CFR 1.7(a)"
{
  new-years-day: fixed(1, 1) effective [1894-06-28, ..) observed nearest_weekday
  inauguration-day: every(4, 1965, 1, 20) effective [1966-09-06, ..)
  // § 6103(c)'s Sunday shift, spelled out: the engine has only the
  // nearest-weekday observance. These are the Sunday Inauguration Days from
  // 2024 to 2100.
  inauguration-day-observed-2041: once(2041-01-21) effective [1966-09-06, ..)
  inauguration-day-observed-2069: once(2069-01-21) effective [1966-09-06, ..)
  inauguration-day-observed-2097: once(2097-01-21) effective [1966-09-06, ..)
  mlk-day: nth(3, monday, 1) effective [1986-01-01, ..)
  washingtons-birthday: nth(3, monday, 2) effective [1971-01-01, ..)
  memorial-day: last(monday, 5) effective [1971-01-01, ..)
  juneteenth: fixed(6, 19) effective [2021-06-17, ..) observed nearest_weekday
  independence-day: fixed(7, 4) effective [1894-06-28, ..) observed nearest_weekday
  labor-day: nth(1, monday, 9) effective [1894-06-28, ..)
  columbus-day: nth(2, monday, 10) effective [1971-01-01, ..)
  veterans-day: fixed(11, 11) effective [1978-01-01, ..) observed nearest_weekday
  thanksgiving-day: nth(4, thursday, 11) effective [1941-12-26, ..)
  christmas-day: fixed(12, 25) effective [1894-06-28, ..) observed nearest_weekday
}

/// The legal holidays of Delaware (1 Del. C. § 501(a)) that `us-federal-holidays`
/// does not already close: Good Friday, the Friday after Thanksgiving and the
/// biennial General Election day. FRCP 6(a)(6)(C) and FRCrimP 45(a)(6)(C)
/// make them legal holidays of the District of Delaware for a period counted
/// forward only, so they are read as `forward_holidays` (core's
/// `frcp6-delaware` and `frcrimp45-delaware`). The other § 501
/// days are federal holidays too, observed on the same days (§ 501(b) moves a
/// Saturday holiday to Friday and a Sunday one to Monday, as § 6103(b)(1)
/// and Executive Order 11582 do; the three days here are never on a
/// weekend); Saturdays (§ 501(a)(11)) are closed by `weekend`.
///
/// The engine has no Easter or "day after" rule, so each day is listed with
/// `once`, from May 2, 2011 (the earliest date a D. Del. package needs: the
/// criminal plan's 2011 amendment) to the end of 2050. The vendored text's
/// credits list no act after 83 Del. Laws, c. 257, of the 2021-2022 General
/// Assembly, which only numbered § 501(a)'s list and added Juneteenth; and
/// none between 77 Del. Laws, c. 328 (2010) and 83 Del. Laws, c. 37 (2021),
/// whose § 1 reprints § 501(a) as it then stood with all three days
/// (sources/de-code/83-del-laws-c-37.txt). So the three days were
/// holidays throughout the span. A day before or after it is not closed, so
/// a last day on it is the earlier date, never a later one. The General
/// Election is held on the federal election day of even years; the Title 15
/// section fixing it is not vendored. Not modeled: Return Day (§ 501(a)(13)), a
/// holiday in Sussex County only and only after noon; the 2 floating
/// holidays of § 501(e), which are leave for State employees, not days; and
/// days the Governor appoints, which no vendored source lists.
calendar delaware-holidays effective [2011-05-02, ..) cite "1 Del. C. § 501(a)" historical
{
  // § 501(a)(3). Good Friday 2011 (April 22) precedes the span.
  good-friday-2012: once(2012-04-06) effective [2011-05-02, ..)
  good-friday-2013: once(2013-03-29) effective [2011-05-02, ..)
  good-friday-2014: once(2014-04-18) effective [2011-05-02, ..)
  good-friday-2015: once(2015-04-03) effective [2011-05-02, ..)
  good-friday-2016: once(2016-03-25) effective [2011-05-02, ..)
  good-friday-2017: once(2017-04-14) effective [2011-05-02, ..)
  good-friday-2018: once(2018-03-30) effective [2011-05-02, ..)
  good-friday-2019: once(2019-04-19) effective [2011-05-02, ..)
  good-friday-2020: once(2020-04-10) effective [2011-05-02, ..)
